>> title: Charred Broccoli
>> category: Vegetables & Sides
>> tags: broccoli, roast, sheet pan, lemon, side
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: charred broccoli, roasted broccoli, broccoli with lemon, blistered broccoli, broccoli spears, simple garlic broccoli
>> pairs-with: lemon-rice, romesco
>> servings: 4
>> time: 40 min
>> step.1: Dry, hot, and not crowded. Wet broccoli steams, a cool pan stews, and a crowded pan does both.
>> step.2: cut into spears with one flat face, air-dry 10 min
>> step.3: toss in oil and salt
>> step.4: roast 500°F (260°C) 14 min, flat face down
>> step.5: stir the lemon-garlic oil
>> step.6: toss off the sheet

Three things, and only three: dry, hot, and not crowded. Wet broccoli steams, a cool pan stews, and a crowded pan does both.

Cut @broccoli{1 1/2%lb}(700 g; crowns and peeled stalks) top to bottom into spears so each one keeps a flat cut face, then spread them on a towel and ~dry{10%min}. Washed broccoli carries more water in its florets than you would believe; put it wet on a hot pan and the first four minutes are spent boiling that off, which is four minutes the pan is not browning anything.

Toss @&(~1)dried broccoli{} with @olive oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL) and @kosher salt{1%tsp}, working the oil into the cut faces with your hands rather than pouring it over the top.

Roast @&(~1)oiled broccoli{} flat face down on a #sheet pan{} at 500°F (260°C) ~roast{14%min}, with a finger's width of bare metal around every spear. Two pans is right and one crowded pan is wrong: steam that cannot leave sits between the pieces, and the difference shows up as grey-green instead of brown. The florets will go dark and papery at the tips — that is not burnt, it is the best part.

Stir @garlic{2%cloves}(grated), @lemon zest{1%tsp}, @lemon juice{1%Tbs}(15 mL), @extra-virgin olive oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) and @red chile flakes{1/2%tsp} in a bowl big enough to hold the broccoli.

Tip @&(~2)charred broccoli{} straight off the sheet into @&(~1)lemon-garlic oil{} and toss while it is hot enough to sizzle. Raw garlic on hot broccoli cooks just enough to lose its rasp; the same garlic on the pan for fourteen minutes at 500°F is black.
